INSERT INTO posts(title,body,date) VALUES('How to Back Up Your Own TikTok, Instagram and YouTube Videos','Your videos are your work. If an account is hacked, suspended or deleted, you can lose years of content overnight. A simple backup routine takes only a few minutes.

Start with the official tools. TikTok lets you request a copy of your data from the account settings, under Download your data. Instagram offers Download your information in the Accounts Center. YouTube creators can export their videos through Google Takeout. These exports contain your original uploads and are the safest option because the platforms provide them.

Keep the originals too. Videos you recorded on your phone or edited on your computer are higher quality than anything downloaded back from a platform, which is compressed. Store them in two places, for example an external drive and a cloud folder.

Name your files clearly. Use the date and a short title, such as 2026-10-23-travel-vlog. When you have hundreds of clips, good names save hours.

If you need to save a public video that you own and no longer have the original file, a link-based tool like Ferryclip can help. Only use it for content you created or have permission to use.

Finally, repeat the backup regularly. Once a month is enough for most creators. Put a reminder in your calendar and treat it like any other routine task.','2026-10-23');
INSERT INTO posts(title,body,date) VALUES('What Is a Good Engagement Rate on Instagram and TikTok?','Engagement rate shows how many people actually interact with a creator’s posts compared with how many follow them. It is one of the first numbers brands check before working with an influencer.

The most common formula is simple: add the likes and comments (and shares, if you track them), divide by the number of followers, then multiply by 100. For example, a post with 800 likes and 40 comments from an account with 20,000 followers has an engagement rate of 4.2 percent.

What counts as good? Benchmarks change by platform, niche and account size, but as a rough guide, below 1 percent is low, 1 to 3 percent is average, 3 to 6 percent is good and above 6 percent is excellent. TikTok accounts often see higher rates than Instagram, and smaller accounts usually have higher rates than very large ones.

Why it matters more than follower count: an account with 10,000 engaged followers can bring a brand better results than an account with 200,000 inactive ones. A sudden jump in followers without a matching rise in likes can also be a warning sign of fake or purchased followers.

You can test your own numbers with the Engagement Rate Calculator on Ferryclip. For a fuller picture, average the last 10 to 20 posts instead of using a single one, and compare with similar accounts in your niche.','2026-10-23');
INSERT INTO posts(title,body,date) VALUES('Copyright Basics for Creators: What You Can and Cannot Download','Not every public video is free to save and reuse. Understanding a few basics helps you avoid takedowns, account strikes and legal trouble.

Content you created yourself is yours. Saving a copy of your own videos for backup or to repost on another platform is generally fine, although you should still follow each platform’s rules.

Content made by someone else is usually protected by copyright, even if it is public and free to watch. Downloading it for private viewing may be tolerated in some places, but sharing, reposting or using it in your own videos without permission can be infringement.

Permission can come in several forms. A creator may give you written consent, publish the video under an open license such as Creative Commons, or offer a media kit for brands. Always check the exact terms, because some licenses forbid commercial use.

Fair use and similar exceptions are limited and depend on the country. Short clips used for commentary, criticism or education may qualify, but there is no magic number of seconds that makes a clip safe.

Practical tips: keep proof of permission, credit the creator where required, and remove the content quickly if the owner asks. If you receive a takedown notice, respond promptly and honestly.

At Ferryclip we ask visitors to download only content they own or have permission to use. If you believe something was saved without your permission, contact us through our Copyright page. This article is general information, not legal advice.','2026-10-23');
